From tru64-unix-managers  Tue Apr 17 17:44:39 2001
From: John Losey <jlosey () solucient ! com>
Date: Tue, 17 Apr 2001 17:44:39 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: SUMMARY: Preventing SYSTEM CONFIGURATION at boot time?
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=98752957423687

Many thanks to those who responded, the suggestions were more accurate and
timely than Software Support.  Thanks to LHERCAUD & Dr. Blinn for pointing
me towards the "it" script and its associated /sbin/it.d directory; and to
Alan Davis, Izzet Ergas, Blake Roberts, & Alan for their other suggestions
as well.

It turned out to be a situation where "it" didn't clean up after itself
after our 4.0d to 4.0f installupdate & patch last September.  Our /sbin/it.d
directory looked like this:
excalibur:root# ls -l /sbin/it.d/*
/sbin/it.d/23.d:
total 0
lrwxrwxrwx   1 root     system        16 Apr 14 21:33 c_install ->
../bin/c_install
lrwxrwxrwx   1 root     system        15 Apr 14 21:33 doconfig ->
../bin/doconfig
lrwxrwxrwx   1 root     system        17 Apr 14 21:33 install_ap ->
../bin/install_ap

/sbin/it.d/bin:
total 37
-rwxr-xr-x   1 bin      bin         2069 Apr 12  1999 c_install
lrwxrwxrwx   1 root     system        26 Apr 14 21:33 doconfig ->
../../../usr/sbin/doconfig
-rwxr-xr-x   1 bin      bin         2608 Apr 12  1999 getdate
-rwxr-xr-x   1 bin      bin         1671 Apr 12  1999 getpasswd
-rwxr-xr-x   1 bin      bin         2561 Apr 12  1999 getsysname
-rwxr-xr-x   1 bin      bin         6109 Apr 12  1999 gettimezone
-rwxr-xr-x   1 bin      bin         2529 Apr 12  1999 gitout
-rwxr-xr-x   1 bin      bin         8251 Apr  2  1999 install_ap
-rwxr-xr-x   1 bin      bin         3440 Dec 29  1997 load_kk
-rwxr-xr-x   1 bin      bin         2002 Apr 12  1999 load_usr_pak
-rwxr-xr-x   1 bin      bin         1956 Apr 12  1999 tunesys

/sbin/it.d/data:
total 4
-rw-r--r--   1 root     system      1251 Feb  6 09:04 cinst.data
-rw-r--r--   1 root     system        67 Sep 24  2000 inst.cnfg
-rw-r--r--   1 root     bin          115 Apr 12  1999 options.mask
excalibur:root#

The files in /sbin/it.d/32.d and /sbin/it.d/data (except for options.mask)
should have been cleaned up after it ran.  This does not appear to have been
the case.

Also, in /sbin/inittab, we were booting to Run-Level 4 and had some custom
application start scripts in /sbin/rc4.d:
is:4:initdefault:
ss:Ss:wait:/sbin/rc0 shutdown < /dev/console > /dev/console 2>&1
s0:0:wait:/sbin/rc0 off < /dev/console > /dev/console 2>&1
lsmr:s:sysinit:/sbin/lsmbstartup -b </dev/console >/dev/console 2>&1 ##LSM
lsm:234:wait:/sbin/lsmbstartup -n </dev/console >/dev/console 2>&1 ##LSM
vol:234:wait:/sbin/vol-reconfig </dev/console >/dev/console 2>&1 ##LSM
fs:234:wait:/sbin/bcheckrc < /dev/console > /dev/console 2>&1
kls:Ss:sysinit:/sbin/kloadsrv < /dev/console > /dev/console 2>&1
sysconfig:234:wait:/sbin/init.d/autosysconfig start < /dev/console >
/dev/console 2>&1
update:234:wait:/sbin/update > /dev/console 2>&1
smsync:23:wait:/sbin/sysconfig -r vfs smoothsync-age=30 > /dev/null 2>&1
smsyncS:Ss:wait:/sbin/sysconfig -r vfs smoothsync-age=0 > /dev/null 2>&1
it:234:wait:/sbin/it < /dev/console > /dev/console 2>&1
kmk:34:wait:/sbin/kmknod > /dev/console 2>&1
s2:234:wait:/sbin/rc2 < /dev/console > /dev/console 2>&1
s3:34:wait:/sbin/rc3 < /dev/console > /dev/console 2>&1
s4:4:wait:/sbin/rc4 < /dev/console > /dev/console 2>&1
cons:1234:respawn:/usr/sbin/getty console console vt100
excalibur:root#

While it was listed as running when booting to RL4, it appears to have been
aware that it wasn't going to RL3 and so it didn't run.

I restored the server in question (an 8400) to a PersonalWorkstation 500au
and booted to genvmunix for testing the fixes.  The fix turns out to be to
cleanup the files in /sbin/it.d and the system then continues to boot
normally and not try to reconfigure itself.  We're also going to return the
default run-level to 3 so as not to confuse 'it' any further.

Thanks again for the assistance.  Once again, this list has proven much more
effective than Software Support.

John Losey


-----Original Message-----
From: John Losey [mailto:jlosey@solucient.com]
Sent: Monday, April 16, 2001 11:20 AM
To: Tru64 UNIX Managers Group (E-mail)
Subject: Preventing SYSTEM CONFIGURATION at boot time?


We have a 4.0f system (PK4) that was installed & Patched in September.
We've power-cycled it numerous times since then.  However, this past
weekend, when I went from Single-User Mode to Run-Level 3, after it added
the swap devices, I then saw "*** SYSTEM CONFIGURATION ***" followed by it
doing a configure of my system (just like after the initial OS install).

I can Ctrl-C and cancel the SYSTEM CONFIGURATION and it will continue its
boot normally, if I don't, it will reconfigure all of the subsets (one of
which is LSM) and I'm no longer able to boot because LSM can't find the
disks.

We haven't rebooted in over a month, but since that last reboot, we brought
in an SA whose experience has been mostly with Solaris boxes.  I'm thinking
that he tried to clean something up and whatever he did doesn't work the
same way on Tru64 as it does on Solaris.  Since he claims to have not done
anything that should have caused this problem, we're stuck trying to find
out what causes the System Configuration to be invoked and put back into
place whatever is necessary for it to not be invoked.

The console session follows.

Thanks,

John Losey



